    {
      "title": "Swatching is Not Optional!",
      "slug": "swatching-is-not-optional",
      "displayOrder": 6,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 230,
        "lessonOrder": 6
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div class=\"handwriting-accent\">You are making progress!</div> ... we've spent a lot of time planning, practicing and thinking about knitting ... are you ready to do some \"real knitting\"?<br><strong>DON'T SKIP THIS SECTION!</strong> <br>Your success with this course AND your success with your knitting machine depends on your understanding and mastering the art of knitting gauge swatches.<br>Without a swatch we are knitting in the dark.\n<ul>\n<li>How is the final sweater going to feel in the yarn and stitch pattern you've chosen?</li>\n<li>Do you have enough yarn?</li>\n<li>Do you have enough needles on your machine for the size you chose?</li>\n\n\n</ul>\n\n\n<div class=\"text-center\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"></p><div class=\"handwriting\" style=\"text-align: center;\">SWATCHING IS NOT OPTIONAL<br><br><br></div><p></p>\n</div>\n<br><a href=\"/downloads/swatch_exercises2.pdf\" class=\"kbm-btn kbm-btn-primary\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Download Swatch Exercises</a>\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4486,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "178357273",
              "title": "'Proper' Gauge Swatches",
              "legacyComponentId": 4514,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Your Success Depends on this",
          "slug": "your-success-depends-on-this",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3307,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<h2>Gauge Swatch vs. Tension Swatch</h2>\n\n<p>Before you start your blanket, it's worth knitting a small sample. It can save time, frustration, and even yarn.</p>\n\n<p>Although many knitters use the terms interchangeably, <strong>a gauge swatch and a tension swatch serve different purposes.</strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"kin-two-column\">\n    <div>\n        <h3>Gauge Swatch</h3>\n\n        <p>A <strong>gauge swatch</strong> is the swatch you use to accurately measure your stitch and row gauge.</p>\n\n        <p>For the best results, your swatch should:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n            <li>Be larger than the area you'll measure.</li>\n            <li>Be finished the same way your project will be finished (washed or steamed if appropriate).</li>\n            <li>Be measured after it has relaxed.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <blockquote class=\"blockquote_style\">\n            <strong>Remember:</strong> The goal of a gauge swatch is accurate measurement.\n        </blockquote>\n    </div>\n\n    <div>\n        <img src=\"/images/courses/swatch_measure.jpg\" alt=\"Measuring stitch and row gauge on a swatch\">\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"kin-two-column\">\n    <div>\n        <h3>Tension Swatch</h3>\n\n        <p>A <strong>tension swatch</strong> is your opportunity to experiment.</p>\n\n        <p>When you're using a new yarn, don't worry about measuring gauge right away. Instead, knit several sections using different tension settings and compare the fabric.</p>\n\n        <p>As you knit, ask yourself:</p>\n\n        <ul>\n            <li>Which tension creates the fabric I like best?</li>\n            <li>Does this yarn look good in stockinette?</li>\n            <li>How does it behave in tuck, slip, lace, or other stitch patterns?</li>\n            <li>Which setting feels right for the project I'm planning?</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <p>Once you've chosen the tension you like, knit a fresh <strong>gauge swatch</strong> using that setting and measure your stitch and row gauge.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div>\n        <img src=\"/images/courses/tension_swatch.jpg\" alt=\"Tension swatch showing several tension settings\">\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Your Turn: Knit a Tension Swatch</h3>\n\n<p>Now it's time to get a feel for your yarn.</p>\n\n<ol class=\"instruction-list\">\n    <li>Cast on about 30 to 40 stitches.</li>\n\n    <li>Begin at the tightest tension that knits comfortably.</li>\n\n    <li>Knit 2 to 4 inches.</li>\n\n    <li>Work a marker row by changing yarn color, threading a contrasting yarn through the stitches, or making a few eyelets.</li>\n\n    <li>Change the tension dial one number looser and knit another section.</li>\n\n    <li>Repeat until you've sampled several tension settings.</li>\n\n    <li>Feel and compare each section to decide which fabric you like best.</li>\n\n    <li>Once you've found your favorite tension, knit a fresh gauge swatch and measure your stitch and row gauge.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<blockquote class=\"blockquote_style\">\n    <strong>Tip:</strong> There isn't a \"correct\" tension. The best tension is the one that creates the fabric you want for your project.\n</blockquote>",
              "legacyComponentId": 4487,
              "order": 3
            }
          ],
          "title": "Gauge swatch verses Tension Swatch",
          "slug": "gauge-swatch-verses-tension-swatch",
          "order": 3,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3308,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
